\subsection{Frontier Model Self-Consistency}\label{app:frontier_sc}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{GPT-5.5 zero-shot SC on Few-NERD ($N{=}8$, 200 instances). Parse failures (10.1\%) excluded from all metrics. Qwen3-8B FT shown for comparison.}
\label{tab:frontier_sc}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcc@{}}
\toprule
Metric & GPT-5.5 ZS & Qwen3-8B FT \\
\midrule
Greedy F1 & .638 & .789 \\
MV F1 & .652 ($+1.4$\,pp) & ${\approx}$.792 \\
Oracle F1 & .720 & ${\approx}$.858 \\
Degeneracy & 50.0\% & ${\approx}$65\% \\
SJ $\rho$ & .346 & .300 \\
Parse failures & 10.1\% & ${\approx}$0\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

GPT-5.5 exhibits the full diagnostic pattern on Few-NERD (Table~\ref{tab:frontier_sc}): high degeneracy (50\%), moderate structural QE (SJ $\rho{=}0.346$, comparable to Qwen fine-tuned), and 8.2\,pp of unrealized oracle headroom despite MV recovering only 1.4\,pp.
This confirms the correlation-selection gap is not specific to open-weight or fine-tuned models.

\paragraph{Parse failure handling.}
10.1\% of GPT-5.5 samples produced output that could not be parsed into valid entity sets.
These samples are excluded from all aggregation metrics (degeneracy, oracle, MV, selection); for instances where all $N$ samples fail parsing, the instance is excluded entirely.
The effective sample size per instance is therefore $N_{\textit{eff}} \leq 8$ (mean ${\approx}7.2$).

